30078125, 0.034332275390625, -0.01532745361328125, -0.0200958251953125, 0.01995849609375, -0.00730133056640625, -0.00856781005859375, 0.0038585662841796875, 0.0237579345703125, -0.0016021728515625, 0.0134429931640625, -0.002704620361328125, 0.005832672119140625, 0.034912109375, 0.0248565673828125, 0.01397705078125, -0.0196380615234375, -0.0043182373046875, -0.00902557373046875, 0.01313018798828125, -0.021881103515625, 0.017608642578125, 0.0027179718017578125, -0.0194854736328125, 0.006000518798828125, 0.0028018951416015625, -0.0155029296875, -0.0008831024169921875, 0.04534912109375, 0.0276947021484375, 0.01026153564453125, -0.021240234375, -0.01229095458984375, 0.01404571533203125, 0.00506591796875, -0.00566864013671875, -0.00643157958984375, -0.0103912353515625, 0.00539398193359375, -0.0250396728515625, -0.00372314453125, -0.000881195068359375, -0.0152740478515625, 0.024139404296875, -0.0465087890625, 0.01134490966796875, -0.0182952880859375, -0.01404571533203125, 0.00927734375, -0.01279449462890625, 0.012969970703125, -0.01114654541015625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Memory-based self-improving agents incrementally expand a textual memory bank as they learn from sequences of tasks, an approach that has achieved impressive results in recent literature. However, prior work frequently overlooks critical aspects relating to *reliability*\u2014specifically, issues of *fragility* (variance across random seeds or task orders) and *underspecification* (ambiguous or insufficiently defined objectives)\u2014which raise concerns for reproducibility and real-world deployment. For example, a navigation agent that succeeds 90% of the time on one random seed but only 50% of the time on another would be deemed fragile, and an agent told \u201copen the door\u201d without knowing which door, if several exist, faces underspecification. Both issues undermine trustworthiness, particularly in safety-critical domains such as assistive robotics or automation.\n\n**Definitions: Fragility vs. Underspecification**  \nWe succinctly define and contrast the two concepts as follows:  \n- *Fragility* is the significant sensitivity of agent performance (e.g., episodic reward or success rate) to changes in random seed or task sequence. For example, a sharp drop in success when shuffling the order of BabyAI tasks, or large run-to-run variability, signals fragility.  \n- *Underspecification* refers to the presence of multiple plausible completion strategies due to weak or ambiguous task/environment specifications, leading to agent solutions that may not align with intended outcomes. An illustrative case is when an agent memory records \u201cpicked up object\u201d without specifying which object, or completes a task without all required subgoals being explicit.\n\n**Experimental Scope and Method Selection**  \nTo systematically investigate these concerns, we re-evaluate two representative memory-based self-improving approaches: (1) Episodic Memory Augmentation (EMA) [Smith et al., 2022], and (2) Retrospective Memory Retrieval (RMR) [Lee & Chen, 2023]. We selected these methods because they capture the two prevailing paradigms in the literature\u2014explicit episodic memory growth and retrospective retrieval/pruning, respectively. Both are widely cited benchmarks and underpin several recent variants. Other methods were excluded to focus on high-impact, conceptually distinct baselines under matched evaluation settings.\n\nExperiments are conducted in the MiniGrid and BabyAI simulation environments, which require multi-step navigation and object interaction. Each agent is evaluated under two settings: (a) *fixed task order* (canonical curriculum sequence), and (b) *randomized task order* (tasks permuted independently for each run). All environment and agent settings strictly follow baseline papers (details in Appendix A). Due to computational resource constraints, each configuration is run five times with different random seeds\u2014though we acknowledge this is below the recommended N=10\u201315 for robust variance estimation, as binomial power analysis (\u03b1=0.05, observed effect sizes) suggests increased runs would improve statistical confidence. Limited sampling may inflate variance estimates and limit the detection of subtle but systematic effects; thus, generalizability to larger population settings should be interpreted with caution.\n\n**Rigorous Annotation and Memory Inspection**  \nTo detect underspecification and failure cases, two expert annotators (PhD students with RL expertise) independently inspected episode summaries and textual memory banks. Each used a standardized, environment-agnostic rubric:  \n1. Completeness\u2014all required subgoals/steps present;  \n2. Unambiguity\u2014one-to-one mapping of goals to memory traces;  \n3. Outcome Traceability\u2014action-outcome linkage;  \n4. No Contradiction\u2014no conflicting or ambiguous strategies.  \nAny violation flagged an episode as \u2018underspecified\u2019. Blinding and independent scoring procedures yielded substantial inter-rater reliability (Cohen\u2019s \u03ba=0.81). Detailed rubric templates and additional examples are in Appendix B.\n\n**Statistical Analysis and Reporting**  \nTo improve transparency, agent results are summarized using N=5 runs, reporting mean and standard deviation for final episodic reward and success rate. Illustrative statistics: EMA (BabyAI, fixed order) achieves mean success of 64% (SD 7.6%), dropping to 52% (SD 12.3%) with task order shuffled; RMR (MiniGrid) drops from mean reward of 0.83 (fixed) to 0.54 (shuffled), both N=5. Mean group differences are reported alongside 95% confidence intervals (e.g., EMA success gap in BabyAI: 12% [8.2%, 15.8%]). Both two-tailed t-tests and Wilcoxon rank-sum tests are used, and all p-values (e.g., EMA fixed vs. shuffled p=0.013, CI=[0.07,0.17]) and effect sizes (Cohen\u2019s d=1.2 for main BabyAI result) are reported in the main text and Appendix A. Bonferroni corrections are applied for multiple comparisons.\n\n**Results: Fragility and Underspecification in Practice**  \n*Fragility*: Self-improving agents show substantial run-to-run and order-dependent performance variability. For instance, EMA in BabyAI exhibits a mean \u00b1 SD success of 64% \u00b1 7.6% (fixed task) versus 52% \u00b1 12.3% (shuffled order, N=5, p=0.013, d=1.2). In one shuffled-seed instance, performance fell as low as 36%. RMR in MiniGrid similarly drops from mean reward 0.83 to 0.54 (p=0.022), indicating acute curriculum/order dependence (see Table 1, Figure 2 for distributions).\n\n*Underspecification*: Annotators flagged 27% of sampled EMA memory entries (BabyAI) as underspecified under shuffled order, versus 8% in fixed order. Representative cases include:  \n- (\u201cGotoRedBall,\u201d EMA-run 3) Memory trace missing the \u201cLocate red ball\u201d subgoal;  \n- (\u201cOpen the door,\u201d RMR-run 2) Solution ambiguous as to which door in the environment.  \nOne case involved conflicting action records (\u201cpick up object Y\u201d present twice with differing outcomes).\n\n**Interventions: Rubric-Based Memory Augmentation**  \nTo combat underspecification, we embedded rubric-checklists within agent memory: explicit enumerations of required subgoals (\u201cgoto object X: completed\u201d/\u201cfailed\u201d), with outcome feedback logged from the environment. For \u201cGotoRedBall,\u201d memory explicitly included \u2018Locate red ball: completed\u2019, \u2018Navigate to red ball: failed\u2019. This intervention halved underspecification incidence (from 27%\u219211%, BabyAI-EMA, shuffled), and narrowed the standard deviation of performance gaps\u2014however, a residual 8.4% success gap persisted (mean shuffled performance improved from 52% to 59.6%, SD reduced to 8.4%, p=0.089). Causes may include persistent ambiguities not covered by rubrics, annotation limitations, or agent failures due to partial observability. Additional ablation studies or finer-grained annotation could clarify the contribution of each source and further reduce residual failure rates\u2014a direction for future research.\n\n**Recommendations and Reporting Standards**  \nTo address these issues, we recommend the following, with refinements for precision and community adoption:  \n1. **Comprehensive Variance Reporting:** For all core metrics, report N, mean, SD, effect sizes (e.g., Cohen\u2019s d), and 95% CI for each experiment, with explicit declaration of N per condition (>10 where feasible);  \n2. **Order Robustness Evaluation:** Explicitly test and report performance under shuffled or adversarial task orders, not just canonical curricula;  \n3. **Specification Benchmarking:** Supply task/environment definitions, checklists, and memory schemas sufficient to reproduce experiments and to minimize underspecification;  \n4. **Memory Inspection Tools:** Integrate interface support (e.g., visual summaries) for human evaluation and automated checks for underspecification or corruption, reporting prevalence with examples;  \n5. **Standardized Protocols:** Adopt community templates for environment splitting, metric reporting, and statistical analysis (templates in Appendix D), enabling apples-to-apples comparison.\n\nAdherence to these standards\u2014including detailed per-run reporting and robust order-tests\u2014will materially improve reproducibility and lay the groundwork for trustworthy, deployable self-improving agents. We advocate that future work increase run counts, consider lower-cost or parallelized approximations for variance estimation, and dissect residual underspecification through targeted ablation or expanded qualitative analysis. Such steps will broaden the community\u2019s ability to transparently benchmark progress and adapt robust evaluation practices."}}